ths from now the project has accumulated real runs, and a raw payload containing an absolute path or token has been committed inside a .anchor/ pointer-record, leaking it into git history before any ship-time scan would have caught it.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"Per the .anchor/README, the C10 no-leak boundary is enforced at commit time only by static .gitignore globs, while the content scan that 'refuses to ship anything under .anchor/' runs at distribution-build time (Wave 8) -- not at every commit. Anything that lands in a tracked path but is misclassified (e.g. a pointer-record that inlines raw fields) can be committed long before the ship-time scan exists to catch it.\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bounded premortem: the gap between 'gitignore decides what is committed' and 'content scan decides what is shipped' is a coming problem the author has not closed. The defense is real but late-bound.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Commit-time leak risk: C10 relies on path globs at commit but content inspection only at ship.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"a2\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"rung\": \"CLAIMED\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"A user hand-edits Anchor.md (e.g. adds notes) and the next Anchor scan silently overwrites those edits, losing the user's content.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"Anchor.md is auto-generated and re-stamped each scan ('Last scanned: ...'), yet it is presented as an ordinary committed, 'safe to commit' markdown file with only a prose note that it is auto-generated -- there is no machine guard (read-only marker / managed-region fences) preventing or preserving manual edits.\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"reasoning\": \"As the project becomes a place people look, the human instinct to annotate the top-level status file collides with its regenerate-on-scan lifecycle. The conservative variant is no guard (silent loss); the aggressive variant is managed-region fences that preserve a user block. Naming the assumption lets the author choose.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Auto-regenerated Anchor.md can silently clobber manual user edits.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nitpicks\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"n1\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"readme.txt contains only 'hi' -- a placeholder with no information about the project. Harmless now, but it is the only human-authored content file and conveys nothing.\",\n      \"severity\": \"trivial\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"n2\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"CLAUDE.md ('R&D project managed by Anchor -- research/plan/build via the trio.') asserts trio management but binds nothing concrete; it reads as aspirational scaffolding text rather than an actionable instruction.\",\n      \"severity\": \"trivial\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"elevations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"e1\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"medium\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Forward, value-adding suggestion tied to d2: have the Anchor bootstrap commit the tracked tier (Anchor.md plus the .anchor/projects/<id> pointer store) as part of registration, so the advertised C9 durability ('durable across restarts') holds from t=0 rather than from the first manual commit. This converts a stated guarantee into an enforced one.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If Anchor intentionally defers the first commit until a real run produces a non-empty pointer-record -- on the rationale that an empty scaffold has nothing durable to protect and committing it just adds churn -- then committing at bootstrap adds noise without durability value, and the current behavior is correct by design.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Commit the C9 tracked tier at bootstrap so durability holds from registration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"e2\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"high\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"CLAIMED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Forward suggestion tied to a1: enforce C10 with a content-scan pre-commit hook (reject any staged .anchor/ payload containing absolute paths, tokens, or PII), not only with .gitignore globs plus a ship-time scan. This closes the commit-time-vs-ship-time window so a misclassified raw payload cannot enter history in the first place.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If, by construction, every code path that writes under .anchor/ routes raw payloads exclusively into gitignored raw/ directories and pointer-records are schema-validated to exclude free-form/raw fields, then a commit-time content scan is redundant and adds friction with no marginal leak reduction.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Add a commit-time content scan to enforce C10, not just gitignore + ship-time scan.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"situate_skip\": {\n    \"performed\": false,\n    \"reason\": \"FIRING GATE not met: no central load-bearing claim in an empty scaffold and STAKES < medium. SITUATE skipped (not degraded). Any best-in-class cross-field framing here would be unverified and is deliberately not asserted.\"\n  }\n}\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":40542,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":278},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91051fd8-156f-43a1-ad24-d388bd96f59d","uuid":"4144d32c-3497-4d10-80d7-f58e40e6f40f","request_id":"req_011CcMTA5WRxxNxx7Hovfvbf"}
